.\" secret-patterns(5) -- an operator's secret-name patterns for Agent Tools Restricted.
.\" @AI_TOOLS_VERSION@ is substituted at deploy time (install.sh install_subst
.\" and the RPM %prep), like ai-tools(1). The matcher described here is the one
.\" secret-patterns.lib.sh implements for every helper that classifies a name.
.TH SECRET-PATTERNS 5 "" "ai-tools @AI_TOOLS_VERSION@" "Agent Tools Restricted"
.SH NAME
secret-patterns \- the file names an operator's sandbox treats as credentials
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ~/.config/ai-tools/secret-patterns
.SH DESCRIPTION
Each operator keeps one
.B secret-patterns
file.
A path whose base name matches a pattern in it is a credential file, and the
sandbox keeps it out of the agent's reach:
.TP
.B on write
A secret-named file the agent creates is quarantined the moment it is written:
owned by you at mode
.BR 0600 ,
which the agent can neither read nor edit in place.
The session receives a notice naming the path.
.TP
.B on claim, and on demand
.B "ai-tools --lockdown"
walks a project and seals every secret-named file already in it the same way,
before the agent is granted access.
.B "ai-tools --project-claim"
and
.B "ai-tools --sandbox-create"
run that scan first.
.PP
The root helpers read this file on your behalf.
It is owned by you at mode
.B 0600
inside a
.B 0700
directory, so the sandbox account can neither read it nor change what it
classifies.
.PP
.B "ai-tools-admin operators add"
creates it with a header and no pattern, which leaves the built-in baseline in
force (see
.BR "THE BASELINE" ).
.SH GRAMMAR
One pattern per line.
A pattern is a glob matched against a file's
.B base name
only, so it does not contain a
.BR / .
Matching is case-insensitive:
.B .env
also matches
.BR .ENV .
.TP
.B *
matches any run of characters, including none.
.TP
.B ?
matches one character.
.TP
.BI [ set ]
matches one character from the set.
.PP
A
.B .
is literal.
A line beginning with
.B #
is a comment; blank lines and surrounding whitespace are ignored.
.SH THE BASELINE
The shared library
.I /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/secret-patterns.lib.sh
carries a built-in list: the names credential files carry across software in
general, kept current by package upgrades.
.PP
.B "A pattern listed in this file replaces that baseline rather than adding to it."
A file holding one pattern classifies on that pattern alone.
To add a name of your own, copy the baseline entries you keep from the library
and write your own beside them.
.PP
When this file is missing, or holds only comments, the baseline is in force,
so a fresh enrolment quarantines every name the baseline lists and every name a
later upgrade adds.
A file you have written keeps this host on the patterns it listed then; at each
launch the wrapper logs how the set in force differs from the baseline, naming the
patterns it drops, so a stale copy is visible in the journal:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
journalctl -t claude -g 'secret patterns'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Anchor a pattern to a name or to an environment segment, as the baseline does
.RB ( appsettings.*.json ,
.BR *.Production.* ).
A broad catch-all such as
.B *.*.json
also matches the build artifacts a toolchain must read, and a quarantined
artifact breaks the build.
A pattern matches any file named after its subject as well: a document called
.B secrets.md
matches
.B secrets.*
and becomes unreadable to the agent.
.PP
A credential name that software writes in general, and that the baseline lacks,
belongs upstream, so every host receives it on the next upgrade.
The library is replaced on upgrade, so an edit made there is lost.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/ai-tools/secret-patterns
This file, one per operator.
.TP
.I ~/.config/ai-tools/allowed-projects
The operator's project allowlist, kept beside it with the same ownership.
.TP
.I /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/secret-patterns.lib.sh
The shared matcher and the built-in baseline.
.SH EXAMPLES
Quarantine a deployment-specific name and keep three baseline entries:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
# names the agent must not read
deploy-*.yaml
\&.env
*.pem
appsettings.*.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Review what a project holds before granting access, then seal it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ai\-tools \-\-lockdown \-\-dry\-run /home/op/webapp
ai\-tools \-\-lockdown /home/op/webapp
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ai\-tools (1),
.BR allowed\-projects (5),
.BR glob (7),
.BR operator.conf (5)
